<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Revolutionary Armed Forces of Colombia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1964">1964</a>–<a href="/years/2017">2017</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as FARC, FARC-EP</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Revolutionary Armed Forces of Colombia (FARC) was a Marxist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> group founded in 1964 by Manuel Marulanda and others from peasant self-defence groups. Increasingly financed by kidnapping and the drug trade, it fought the Colombian state for more than five decades. After a peace agreement signed in 2016 with President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-santos">Juan Manuel Santos</a> it disarmed and became a political party, though dissident groups fought on.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/revolutionary-armed-forces-of-colombia.jpg" alt="Revolutionary Armed Forces of Colombia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MrPenguin20 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_FARC-EP.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The group grew out of peasant self-defence groups formed from 1948, during the period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-violencia">La Violencia</a>, and promoted agrarianism and anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a>. The Colombian Communist Party, accredited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a> in 1930, organised peasant leagues and communist-controlled self-defence communities in the south that could resist state forces. In the 1960s the government's Accelerated Economic Development policy favoured large export farms; thousands of peasants were evicted, and the number of landless families rose from 40,000 in 1961 to 400,000 by 1969.</p><p>In the early 1960s the government began attacking the communist-held territories. After a military assault on the community of Marquetalia, in which about 16,000 troops faced 48 armed fighters, Manuel Marulanda Vélez and 47 others escaped into the mountains. These 48 men, two of them women, formed the core of the FARC.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At its Seventh Guerrilla Conference in 1982 the group decided to become an irregular army able to stage large attacks, helped by income from the coca boom, and added the letters EP, for Ejército del Pueblo or People's Army, to its name. In November 1998 President Andrés Pastrana granted it a safe haven of 42,000 square kilometres around San Vicente del Caguán in peace talks. Under President Álvaro Uribe, from 2002, military pressure forced it into a strategic withdrawal, and many fighters and commanders were captured or deserted. By 2014 it was avoiding open combat with the army and concentrating on small ambushes.</p><p>Its strength varied: 18,000 members by its own claim in 2007, and fewer than 10,000 according to President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-santos">Juan Manuel Santos</a> in 2011. It was financed by kidnapping for ransom, extortion, taxation of economic activity including the drug trade, and mining. The state, right-wing paramilitaries and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> groups also committed atrocities in the conflict.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Human Rights Watch, Amnesty International and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> accused the group of violations including murders of civilians, hostage-taking, the use of landmines and gas cylinder mortars, and the recruitment of children. Women made up perhaps a third of its members, and the group formally banned rape, though former members have reported forced abortions.</p><p>In June 2016 the FARC signed a ceasefire with Santos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>. A referendum on the first accord failed on 2 October, with 50.24 percent against, and a revised deal was signed in November and approved by Congress. On 27 June 2017 the FARC handed its weapons to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, and soon afterwards reformed as a legal political party, later renamed the Commons. Dissident groups of about 2,000 to 2,500 people continued to use its doctrine.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Revolutionary Armed Forces of Colombia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the 1960s the armed forces fought guerrilla movements, among them the <b>FARC</b> and the ELN, both formed in 1964, and the M-19, whose seizure of the Palace of Justice in November 1985 ended in an army assault that left about a hundred dead.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-colombia">Republic of Colombia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
